 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Nov 24, 2026 
 
 
 With a flurry of economic data due out tomorrow, we're bringing you our annual Thanksgiving tradition a day early: five economic trends that make us thankful. 🦃

 Situational awareness: Consumer confidence surged this month to its highest level since early last year, the Conference Board said . The research group attributed the 12.4% gain in its index to the resolution of the midterm elections and easing gasoline prices.

 Separately, Bloomberg reports that the Federal Reserve's inspector general is investigating whether vice chair for supervision Michelle Bowman violated communication rules by appearing at a bank-sponsored event during the central bank's monetary policy blackout period. Today's newsletter, edited by Jeffrey Cane and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 1,135 words, a 4.5-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Five reasons for economic gratitude 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios. Photo: Library of Congress

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Not all the economic surprises of 2026 have been unpleasant. Some developments offer good reason to be optimistic about the years ahead. 

 Here are five we are grateful for ahead of Thanksgiving . 1. The productivity revival After years of lackluster productivity growth, the U.S. economy is getting better at producing more with the same amount of labor. Stronger productivity growth creates room for businesses to raise wages without raising prices, and for the economy to grow faster without fueling inflation.

 The productivity numbers are already encouraging , even before the transformative economic benefits promised by AI are clearly visible in the data. Labor productivity has grown at a 2.5% annual pace since early 2023, according to San Francisco Fed research. That's well above the 1.5% pace that prevailed from 2005 to 2022, though short of the 3.3% recorded during the technology boom of the late 1990s and early 2000s. That has obviously come with some existential angst about the future of human labor in the era of AI. But we spent too much time handwringing about the weak productivity gains of the 2010s — and related sluggish growth in incomes — not to view this shift with optimism. 2. The entrepreneurship boom Americans are applying to start new businesses at the fastest pace on record, with more than 5 million applications filed in the first 10 months of this year, according to Census Bureau data.

 There are early clues that AI may be fueling a new wave of entrepreneurship by making it easier to launch and run a business without employees. By the numbers: Businesses less likely to hire employees accounted for 86% of the increase in new business applications in the first 10 months of the year, compared with the same period in 2025 — a sign of a burgeoning "solopreneur" economy, though some of these categorized businesses may eventually hire workers.

 The boom is also evident in data from payments company Stripe. The number of new businesses launching on its platform each week has roughly doubled from a year ago, CEO Patrick Collison said last week at a Fed conference — a rate of growth without precedent in Stripe's 15-year history. 3. Low, low layoffs This time a year ago, there was deep angst over the outlook for the labor market , as companies looked to an AI-powered future and the unemployment rate edged up.

 But not only has the headline unemployment rate held steady at low levels this year, but the rate at which companies are laying people off is near all-time lows. State of play: The rate at which employers laid off or discharged workers was 1% in September, only a tick above the 0.9% low in a data series that dates to 2001.

 That rate averaged 1.2% in 2019, which was then viewed as a healthy labor market. Weekly data on unemployment insurance claims tell the same story. The four-week moving average of initial claims has been below 200,000 in recent weeks. Those are the lowest levels on record in a data series dating to 1967 — a time when the labor force was much smaller. Zoom out: Getting a job is challenging in some sectors, as hiring rates are relatively low, especially in white-collar professions. But those who have a job have been able to hold onto it this year at historically high rates.

 
 
