 2. Two more for the road ... 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Bureau of Labor Statistics and Energy Information Administration; Chart: Neil Irwin/Axios 4. People can handle high gas prices better There's no doubt that gasoline prices have risen a lot since the Iran war began in March. But households are better positioned to handle those costs than they were in the past.

 Zoom in: The average nationwide price of a gallon of gasoline was $3.64 last week, per the Energy Information Administration — up from $2.94 in late February.

 But $4 gasoline is a considerably smaller burden relative to wages and overall spending than it was in past energy shocks. We like to look at gasoline prices in terms of how long a typical laborer — someone making the average hourly earnings for production and nonsupervisory workers — would have to work to afford a gallon.

 In October, that figure was just shy of 8 minutes. That was nearly 11 minutes during the onset of the Ukraine war in 2022. It was at or above 10 minutes for most of the period from early 2011 to the fall of 2014, as well as from the spring of 2007 to the fall of 2008. Of note: Energy is also a smaller share of household spending than it once was, reflecting greater energy efficiency of modern cars and household appliances.

 In September, gasoline and other energy goods were 2.4% of total personal consumption expenditures. Twenty years ago, that figure was 3.5%. 5. America's financial dominance The U.S. remains at the center of the global financial system , giving American companies an extraordinary ability to raise money to finance growth and innovation.

 Set aside the day-to-day swings in Anthropic's stock price: The 5-year-old company pulled off the largest IPO in history last week, raising more than $90 billion for its own coffers before fees. The two largest IPOs in world history took place in the U.S. this year: SpaceX and Anthropic. Two episodes this year suggest investors still turn to traditional U.S. safe-haven assets in times of turmoil.

 The dollar rallied following the outbreak of the Iran war, and it's the strongest since before President Trump's "liberation day" tariffs in April 2025. Long-term Treasury yields fell during last week's stock market sell-off in a classic flight to safety, despite stubborn inflation and the prospect of further Fed rate hikes. America's AI investment boom is helping sustain its growth advantage. The International Monetary Fund, for instance, projects the U.S. economy will grow 2.3% next year — faster than any other G7 nation.

 The bottom line: The U.S. economy has plenty of problems, and nobody would claim that all is perfect. But there are striking areas of resilience and strength that we can be grateful for this Thanksgiving.

 
 
